      "rev_n_6_67b3bd_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_67b3bd_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_67b3bd",
        "thought_text": "Dynamic programming: Calculate minimum moves needed for each prefix and suffix",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-4257421872844321723",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794528135.4123287
      },
      "rev_n_4_b9ab42_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_b9ab42_1",
        "node_id": "n_4_b9ab42",
        "thought_text": "The current approach of moving '1's to the left is not optimal. A better strategy would be to move '0's to the right, as this will result in a lexicographically smaller string. This approach works because moving '0's to the right preserves the relative order of '1's, which is crucial for maintaining the lexicographically smallest result.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-4257421872844321723",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_4_b9ab42_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_0f0e10393d",
        "created_at": 1794528244.3867621
      },
      "rev_n_5_e81e0b_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_e81e0b_1",
        "node_id": "n_5_e81e0b",
        "thought_text": "The previous approach using a two-pointer technique was flawed because it didn't correctly handle cases where multiple swaps were needed to move a '1' to the left. Instead, a more effective strategy is to use a greedy approach that focuses on moving '1's to the left as much as possible within the given number of moves. This approach ensures that we always make the most impactful swaps first, leading to the lexicographically smallest result.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-4257421872844321723",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_5_e81e0b_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_6a1eec0a9a",
        "created_at": 1794528299.1292791
